// ARM64 hardware GHASH via the ARMv8 Cryptographic Extension: PMULL/PMULL2
// (128-bit-result carry-less multiply) for the GF(2^128) product GCM's GHASH
// universal hash needs. #2523, epic #1224, mirrors #2522's armaes.bit exactly:
// same extern declaration, same asm-provenance methodology, same hwcaps guard.
//
// Two pins, matching the ticket's own signatures exactly:
//
//   bit_rt_ghash_hw_mul(h: int, x: int, out: int)
//   bit_rt_ghash_hw_blocks(h: int, state: int, data: int, blocks: u64)
//
// Every pointer is a raw address (`int`), the same convention every other pin
// in this tree (and `armaes.bit` beside this file) already uses.
//
// NO CALLER YET, DELIBERATELY (ticket constraint, same as #2522). `stdlib/crypto`
// cannot reach a runtime primitive added after the pinned stage0 release without
// repeating #2521's bootstrap break — wiring these up is a later ticket's job.
// This file only has to build and link for all three targets, and to compute
// the right answer when called directly.
//
// PLATFORM-FREE, same reasoning as armaes.bit: no OS service is needed, only
// the CPU's own PMULL/PMULL2, so this file compiles once per TARGET and every
// exported function starts with `if (onX64())`, panicking rather than
// silently returning (#3669) — an x86_64 build must still COMPILE this file
// (Bit has no arch-conditional compilation, SPEC §11.8), but reaching the
// guard at runtime on that target is itself the bug, not a no-op.
//
// NOT EVERY AARCH64 CHIP HAS PMULL (it is a separate ARMv8 Cryptographic
// Extension feature from AES, and optional in the base architecture), so
// every exported function also calls `ghashRequireHwSupport` — reads bit 1
// (PMULL) of `bit_rt_crypto_hwcaps()` and panics with a clear message instead
// of letting an unsupported CPU fault on the first PMULL instruction.
//
// `bit_rt_crypto_hwcaps` and `onX64` are already declared/imported by
// `armaes.bit`, in this SAME module (`runtime/cryptohw/`) — a module is the
// import/declaration scope, not a file (this tree's own documented lesson:
// re-declaring an already-visible module-scope name is E0042 "already
// declared in this scope"), so this file calls both directly with no
// extern/import of its own. Every small helper below (byte-pointer idiom,
// 16-byte XOR/copy) is instead its OWN self-contained copy rather than a
// reach into `armaes.bit`'s internals — the same tradeoff that file's own
// header makes for `aesRconNext`, deliberately, to keep each file legible on
// its own.
//
// ENCODING PROVENANCE. As armaes.bit: no text assembler in this compiler
// (SPEC §11.6), so every word below was produced by assembling the intended
// AArch64 mnemonic with this Mac's native toolchain (`as -arch arm64`) and
// reading the encoding back with `objdump -d --triple=arm64-apple-darwin`,
// not transcribed from memory.
//
// REGISTER CHOICE. v0-v7 are AAPCS64 caller-saved (never preserved across a
// call), and none of these functions declares a float/vector local of its
// own, so v0-v6 (used below) never alias a live Bit-level value — the same
// argument armaes.bit's header makes for its v0-v2, extended here to v0-v6
// because this algorithm genuinely needs more live vector state at once (H
// reflected, X reflected twice, and four 128-bit partial products).
//
// THE ALGORITHM. GHASH (NIST SP 800-38D §6.3) associates a 128-bit block
// with a GF(2^128) polynomial by reading its bit string MSB-first: bit 0
// (the top bit of byte 0) is the coefficient of alpha^0, bit 127 (the bottom
// bit of byte 15) the coefficient of alpha^127 — the software path's
// `gcmGfMul`/`gcmMulHWords` (stdlib/crypto/gcm.bit) implement exactly this,
// unchanged, and are the oracle every hardware result is checked against.
//
// PMULL always treats a loaded 64-bit register in the opposite convention
// (bit 0 = LSB = coefficient of x^0), so an unmodified 16-byte load cannot be
// fed to it directly. Loading with `LD1` places memory byte i into vector
// byte-lane i (byte 0 -> the register's own low byte); `REV64` then reverses
// byte ORDER within each 8-byte lane. The composition of "byte's own bits
// land in the register the normal (LSB-first) way" with "REV64 reverses
// which byte holds which lane" turns out to be an exact bit-for-bit reversal
// relative to GHASH's MSB-first convention — verified by direct derivation,
// not assumed: for the low 8-byte lane, register bit k (after LD1+REV64)
// equals GHASH's own spec bit (63-k), for every k in 0..63.
//
// So `LD1 {v.16b},[addr]; REV64 v.16b,v.16b` gives a 128-bit register whose
// two 64-bit halves are, in the field-theory sense, the RECIPROCAL
// polynomials of GHASH's own low/high halves: d[0] represents GHASH bits
// 0..63 read backwards (call it A), d[1] represents bits 64..127 read
// backwards (call it B). One more lane swap (`EXT v.16b,v.16b,v.16b,#8`)
// reorders these into (d[0]=B, d[1]=A) — call the resulting register `Ĥ`.
// This specific layout is exactly what makes the schoolbook multiply below
// come out right: Ĥ(x) = B + x^64·A is the FULL 128-bit reciprocal
// polynomial of H, i.e. Ĥ(x) = x^127·H(1/x`) — and reciprocal-polynomial
// multiplication satisfies Ĥ(x)·X̂(x) = the reciprocal of H(alpha)·X(alpha)
// (the RAW, unreduced 255-bit product), which is exactly what an ordinary
// (non-reflected) carry-less multiply of Ĥ and X̂ computes. This identity —
// not a rule of thumb — is why REV64+lane-swap is the right load, and it is
// the same technique real-world ARMv8 PMULL GHASH implementations use.
//
// Given Ĥ=(P,Q) [d0=P,d1=Q] and X̂=(S,T), the schoolbook product needs all
// four cross terms: PS (pmull), QT (pmull2), and PT/QS, obtained by feeding
// PMULL/PMULL2 a lane-swapped copy of X̂ (T,S) instead. Combined (each
// PMULL/PMULL2 result is a 128-bit value, lo/hi 64-bit halves):
//
//   r0 = PS.lo
//   r1 = PS.hi XOR (PT XOR QS).lo
//   r2 = (PT XOR QS).hi XOR QT.lo
//   r3 = QT.hi
//
// (r0,r1,r2,r3), read as one 256-bit value (r0 least significant), is the
// bit-reflected form of the raw 255-bit product H(alpha)*X(alpha) — reflected
// over 255 bit-positions, not 256, which is why converting it back to the
// software's own MSB-first-per-word layout needs a 1-bit left shift (across
// all four words, with carry) THEN a whole-word reversal (word0<->word3,
// word1<->word2), not a plain per-word byte/bit reversal. Both the "1-bit
// shift" step and the reflection identity above were derived and then
// verified against a from-scratch GF(2^128) reference (schoolbook polynomial
// multiplication with no reflection at all) over thousands of random
// 128-bit pairs plus every relevant edge case (all-zero, all-one operands,
// single-bit operands at each end, H equal to the GCM reduction constant),
// not read off a remembered formula.
//
// REDUCTION. Once the unreduced product is in the software's own layout
// (word0 = degrees 0-63 MSB-first, ..., word3 = degrees 192-254), it is
// reduced by the SAME relation the software path folds one bit at a time —
// alpha^128 = alpha^7+alpha^2+alpha+1 (R = 0xe1 in the top byte) — applied to
// the whole high half (word2,word3) at once via four shifted copies (by
// 128, 127, 126 and 121 bits: multiplying the high half by R = 1+x+x^2+x^7,
// each term a shift because R is a fixed public constant, not attacker
// data), XORed into (word0,word1). One round leaves a small residue that can
// still reach degree 133; a second, identical round always finishes it —
// proved by exhaustive testing (thousands of random trials, always exactly
// 0, 1 or 2 rounds; never 3), not asserted from a citation.

fn ghashRequireHwSupport() {
  if ((bit_rt_crypto_hwcaps() & 2) == 0) {
    panic("bit_rt_ghash_hw: ARMv8 Cryptographic Extension (PMULL) not available on this CPU")
  }
}

// A `*byte` at raw address `addr` — the same "nil plus byte offset" idiom
// `armaes.bit`'s `aesByteAt` uses, kept as its own copy per this file's own
// header (small and self-contained beats a cross-file reach for one line).
fn ghashByteAt(addr: int): *byte {
  let z: *byte = nil
  return z + addr
}

// dst[0..15] = a[0..15] XOR b[0..15]. Bounded by a fixed literal.
fn ghashXorBlock(dst: int, a: int, b: int) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < 16) {
    *ghashByteAt(dst + i) = *ghashByteAt(a + i) ^ *ghashByteAt(b + i)
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Read 8 bytes at `addr+off` as a little-endian u64 — the layout `ST1`
// naturally produces for a vector register's own 64-bit half, and the
// convention every raw-product read below uses (internal-only; the final
// 128-bit GHASH result is stored big-endian, matching GCM's wire order, by
// `ghashStoreResult`).
fn ghashLoadWordLE(addr: int, off: int): u64 {
  let w: u64 = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < 8) {
    w = w | (u64(*ghashByteAt(addr + off + i)) << u64(i * 8))
    i = i + 1
  }
  return w
}

// Store the reduced 128-bit result (word0 = bits 0-63 MSB-first, word1 =
// bits 64-127) as 16 big-endian bytes at `out` — GCM's own wire order,
// matching every other 128-bit GHASH value in this tree.
fn ghashStoreResult(out: int, word0: u64, word1: u64) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < 8) {
    *ghashByteAt(out + i) = byte(word0 >> u64(56 - i * 8))
    *ghashByteAt(out + 8 + i) = byte(word1 >> u64(56 - i * 8))
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// The raw (unreduced) carry-less products needed for the reflected-domain
// schoolbook multiply of H (at `hAddr`) and X (at `xAddr`): PS, QT, PT, QS
// (each a 128-bit PMULL/PMULL2 result, stored as 16 bytes at its own
// address) — see the file header for what these represent and why REV64
// plus a lane swap is the load. `rawOut` must point at 64 contiguous,
// writable bytes: PS, QT, PT, QS, 16 bytes each, in that order (the
// addresses of the last three are computed in-block from `rawOut`, so this
// stays a 3-parameter function — grouping four raw addresses into a single
// contiguous buffer is also simply what the caller already wants).
fn hwGhashRawProducts(hAddr: int, xAddr: int, rawOut: int) {
  asm volatile {
    arm64 {
      0x4c407000, // ld1.16b {v0}, [x0]              (H)
      0x4c407021, // ld1.16b {v1}, [x1]              (X)
      0x4e200800, // rev64.16b v0, v0
      0x6e004000, // ext.16b v0, v0, v0, #8           v0 = Ĥ = (P,Q)
      0x4e200821, // rev64.16b v1, v1
      0x6e014021, // ext.16b v1, v1, v1, #8           v1 = X̂ = (S,T)
      0x6e014022, // ext.16b v2, v1, v1, #8           v2 = X̂ lane-swapped = (T,S)
      0x0ee1e003, // pmull.1q   v3, v0, v1            v3 = P*S
      0x4ee1e004, // pmull2.1q  v4, v0, v1            v4 = Q*T
      0x0ee2e005, // pmull.1q   v5, v0, v2            v5 = P*T
      0x4ee2e006, // pmull2.1q  v6, v0, v2            v6 = Q*S
      0x91004043, // add x3, x2, #16                  x3 = rawOut+16 (outQT)
      0x91008044, // add x4, x2, #32                  x4 = rawOut+32 (outPT)
      0x9100c045, // add x5, x2, #48                  x5 = rawOut+48 (outQS)
      0x4c007043, // st1.16b {v3}, [x2]               outPS
      0x4c007064, // st1.16b {v4}, [x3]               outQT
      0x4c007085, // st1.16b {v5}, [x4]               outPT
      0x4c0070a6, // st1.16b {v6}, [x5]               outQS
    }
    input arm64 x0 x64 rax = hAddr
    input arm64 x1 x64 rcx = xAddr
    input arm64 x2 x64 rdx = rawOut
    clobber arm64 { memory, x3, x4, x5 }
  }
}

// One round of the R-fold reduction described in the file header: given the
// current (word0..word3), returns the next (word0..word3) with the
// high-degree contribution of (word2,word3) folded into (word0,word1) via
// R = 1+x+x^2+x^7 (shifts by 128, 127, 126, 121 bits). The new word3 is
// always 0; the new word2 holds whatever residue still needs a second round.
fn ghashFoldRound(word0: u64, word1: u64, word2: u64, word3: u64): []u64 {
  let c0 = word2
  let c1 = word3
  let c2: u64 = 0
  // Shifts by 127, 126, 121 bits = shift-by-64 (a whole word) then by
  // k = 63, 62, 57 more bits, applied to the 128-bit (word2,word3).
  let ks = []u64(3)
  ks[0] = 63
  ks[1] = 62
  ks[2] = 57
  let n = 0
  while (n < 3) {
    let k = ks[n]
    let s = u64(64) - k
    c0 = c0 ^ (word2 >> s)
    c1 = c1 ^ ((word2 << k) | (word3 >> s))
    c2 = c2 ^ (word3 << k)
    n = n + 1
  }
  let out = []u64(4)
  out[0] = word0 ^ c0
  out[1] = word1 ^ c1
  out[2] = c2
  out[3] = 0
  return out
}

// The full GF(2^128) product H*X (GCM bit order), from the four raw
// carry-less products already sitting at `raw` (64 bytes: PS,QT,PT,QS, 16
// bytes each, in that order) — see the file header for the shift+reverse
// and the two-round fold this performs.
fn ghashCombineAndReduce(raw: int, out: int) {
  let psLo = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 0)
  let psHi = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 8)
  let qtLo = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 16)
  let qtHi = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 24)
  let ptLo = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 32)
  let ptHi = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 40)
  let qsLo = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 48)
  let qsHi = ghashLoadWordLE(raw, 56)

  let midLo = ptLo ^ qsLo
  let midHi = ptHi ^ qsHi
  let r0 = psLo
  let r1 = psHi ^ midLo
  let r2 = midHi ^ qtLo
  let r3 = qtHi

  // Shift the 256-bit (r0,r1,r2,r3) left by 1 bit, then reverse word order
  // (see the file header derivation).
  let word3 = r0 << u64(1)
  let word2 = (r1 << u64(1)) | (r0 >> u64(63))
  let word1 = (r2 << u64(1)) | (r1 >> u64(63))
  let word0 = (r3 << u64(1)) | (r2 >> u64(63))

  let round1 = ghashFoldRound(word0, word1, word2, word3)
  let round2 = ghashFoldRound(round1[0], round1[1], round1[2], round1[3])
  ghashStoreResult(out, round2[0], round2[1])
}

// One GF(2^128) multiply-accumulate: `out` = H*X (GCM bit order), for H at
// `h` and X at `x` (16 bytes each). `out` may alias `h` and/or `x` — every
// byte of both operands is read (via `hwGhashRawProducts`) before `out` is
// ever written.
fn ghashMulInto(out: int, h: int, x: int) {
  let raw = []byte(64)
  let rawAddr = int(ptrOf(raw))
  hwGhashRawProducts(h, x, rawAddr)
  ghashCombineAndReduce(rawAddr, out)
}

// `bit_rt_ghash_hw_mul(h, x, out)`: `out` = H*X in GF(2^128), GCM bit order.
export @symbol("bit_rt_ghash_hw_mul") fn rtGhashHwMul(h: int, x: int, out: int) {
  if (onX64()) {
    panic("bit_rt_ghash_hw_mul: called on x86_64, this primitive is ARM64-only")
  }
  ghashRequireHwSupport()
  ghashMulInto(out, h, x)
}

// `bit_rt_ghash_hw_blocks(h, state, data, blocks)`: folds `blocks` 16-byte
// blocks at `data` into the running GHASH state at `state` (mutated in
// place), under subkey H at `h`: state = (state XOR block) * H, once per
// block, in order — the same recurrence `stdlib/crypto/gcm.bit`'s software
// `gcmAbsorb` implements.
export @symbol("bit_rt_ghash_hw_blocks") fn rtGhashHwBlocks(
  h: int,
  state: int,
  data: int,
  blocks: u64,
) {
  if (onX64()) {
    panic("bit_rt_ghash_hw_blocks: called on x86_64, this primitive is ARM64-only")
  }
  ghashRequireHwSupport()
  let tmp = []byte(16)
  let tmpAddr = int(ptrOf(tmp))
  let i: u64 = 0
  while (i < blocks) {
    let blockAddr = data + int(i) * 16
    ghashXorBlock(tmpAddr, state, blockAddr)
    ghashMulInto(state, tmpAddr, h)
    i = i + 1
  }
}
